let's bring in Mike Florio Pro Football  Talk Pro Football Talk comm Mike joins  me here on the show Mike just your  reaction to the store in the Detroit  news about Matt Patricia I mean it is  amazing Chris in this day and age that  it was kept quiet for so long somebody  referred to it as a skeleton in the  closet this is a scale and that was out  of the closet this is an indictment this  is a public event it's a public record  it's a it's a big deal guilt or  innocence set aside when a grand jury  convenes and decides that there is  enough evidence to conclude that there's  probable cause to believe that the crime  was committed and an indictment comes  and the case is set on a course toward  trial that is a big deal the trial  didn't happen there was no opportunity  for the allegations to be proven or  disproven but the indictment makes it a  big deal that causes it to take on a  higher level of significance and I think  you know you're talking about the lion's  not knowing about this I think there's  just a certain assumption that when a  guy has a public life as a football  coach and he's rising in the ranks and  his name is coming up repeatedly for  head coaching jobs if there was anything  like that out there it would have come  out at some point and you know if you're  gonna dot every I and cross every T you  are gonna find this out but I can  understand why a team like lions would  just assume that if this guy had been  indicted at some point during his adult  life we would know about it by now and  we just simply didn't okay so how big a  problem is this from a Patricia well you  know from a legal standpoint I doubt  that there's any statute of limitations  it hasn't run yet either criminal or  civil so if the alleged victim at this  point has a change of heart I don't  think that's an issue but it's a PR  problem for the Lions for Patricia and  possibly for the league office and we've  seen how the league reacted to the Ray  Rice fiasco from a few years ago we've  seen how the league has handled various  allegations against players it's much  more a PR function than it is at least  in my opinion an effort to get to the  truth and mete out justice the Ezekiel  Elliott was a prime case of PR trumping  the idea of whether or not a guy  actually did what he's accused of doing  because as EQ Elliott never got a fair  chance to defend himself in that setting  so if PR takes over and this does on  fold in a certain way for the Lions you  know if the alleged victim decides to go  on 60 minutes or or ESPN or somewhere  else and sit down and tell her story and  if she tells her story in a convincing  way regardless of whether or not it's  actually true that could be the kind of  thing that creates a snowball effect for  the lion so I think I understand why  they came out with their statements last  night if I was advising Matt Patricia  I'd say you have to give a stirring  passionate convincing shout from the  rooftops defense of yourself today  because that's what people who are  falsely accused do and then we just hope  we can move forward and this dies down  but that that's that's where PR I think  takes a front seat and the legal system  takes a backseat because at this point  it's not about the legal system it's  about the court of public opinion a case  that was never tried in a court of law  potentially is going to be tried in the  court of public opinion and the lions  and Patricia I think at this point among  other things have to hope that the  alleged victim doesn't decide to engage  in that trial in the court of public  opinion the Lions probably should have  done or should have uncovered this know  during their research on Patricia but  does a guy like Patricia going for a job  like this does he have a responsibility  to bring this up to get in front of it  or or no well I I don't think that  there's a responsibility on him to wear  on his sleeve things that would be  readily available if if a basic criminal  background check was done on the guy and  there are certain limitations on what  you should and shouldn't ask and I  remember when I was practicing law there  was kind of a line there between asking  someone about convictions and asking  someone about arrests because a lot of  people get arrested and a lot of those  people never get charged never get  convicted never are found responsible  this is the gray area in between where  you do have a grand jury that has  determined that an indictment was  required and I'm in the process of  getting my hands on that the guidelines  that the NFL gives to the teams about  the things that can and can't be asked  during an interview of a head coach and  I think too this isn't like an  entry-level sales job somewhere I have a  feeling that that as a practical matter  what gets asked of these guys is more  perfunctory and more so  well than what it would be in another  setting because I mean the Lions were  determined to hire Matt Patricia from  the moment Bob Quinn became the GM two  years ago  and Bob Quinn has worked with him Bob  Quinn knows and Bob Quinn isn't in a  position where he's going to do the same  kind of extensive background check that  he would for somebody he doesn't know  and and maybe that's the lesson for  these teams going forward you need to  engage the procedures that are out there  it doesn't take much time it's worth the  extra little bit of elbow grease just to  avoid the embarrassment because in a  minimum even if they had known about it  and they would have hired him anyway  they're in a position where they can  control the narrative Chris and they can  get it out there on their terms instead  of being caught flat-footed like they  were last night when the Detroit News  puts its story out Martha Ford is the  owner of the Detroit Lions she's been a  strong advocate for domestic violence  victims in the past you think that plays  a factor in all this as we move forward  well I think it potentially does but it  all depends upon how big of a reaction  there is I I think this is the point now  where we just sit back and we wait and  we see what kind of a groundswell there  is or there isn't directed at the Lions  directed at the league how what kind of  public pressure is going to be placed on  the Lions or the league to do something  about this and we've seen that in  different situations there is an intense  amount of public pressure that comes to  bear how big of a story is it going to  be are they going to cover it on the  national network news shows or is it  going to be something that's confined to  the sports media I mean these are all  these are all factors that I think for  the lines and the team and then the  league rather they just sit back and  wait and see how it unfolds and how big  of a story it becomes and then I think  you have to react to it  if it does end up becoming a bigger  story and if it becomes if it becomes  too big and there are too many people  who are saying this isn't acceptable  that's when the league office has to  decide whether or not to make a  difficult phone call to the league and  say you guys need to do something about  this but that that's all to be  determined that's just one possible way  this could shake out but if I'm the  Lions right now Chris I mean I've got my  my list of all possible outcomes in  worst case scenario is you know there's  an overwhelming outcry and we're in a  position where we may have to do  something we don't want to do always  great to talk to you Mike I appreciate  the insight all right Chris take care  pal that's Mike  Florio pro football talk pro football  talk calm joining me here on the Dan  Patrick show for more Dan Patrick show  to the audience Channel 239 on DirecTV  or download the Dan Patrick show app  [Music]

